// =============================================================================
// stdlib.lumen — the standard-library pack (shared types + cross-cutting ops)
// =============================================================================
// Two parts: §1 the generalised annotation model (Annotation / Label / Comment),
// and §2 the standard OPERATIONS — search / query / batch / graph, et al. — that
// sit above the core primitive floor (get + list). §1:
//
// The generalised annotation model the issue pack's `computed labels` / `comments`
// projections resolve against. An Annotation is a rev-neutral resource that points
// AT a target resource and carries its own payload; Label and Comment are the two
// concrete annotations the issue pack leans on today, each generalised from a
// single target type to any resource.
//
// This refines the as-needed record landed with #403 (commit 07b65251): the §1
// OPEN is now RESOLVED (annotations are resources that SOFT-reference their target,
// not `rel<>` edges — see §1.0), and the rev-neutrality guarantee is stated
// normatively (§1, PRINCIPLE 1). One question remains, and it belongs to Core, not
// here: Core has no resource-level natural-key constraint yet (`unique` is rel-only),
// so Label's (target, name) key is spelled as intent and flagged CORE-OPEN (§1.2).
//
// SCOPE — what lives here:
//   Annotation — a rev-neutral resource that points AT a target resource and
//   carries its own payload. Label and Comment are the two concrete annotations
//   the issue pack leans on today.
//
// PRINCIPLES:
//   1. Annotations are rev-neutral w.r.t. their target. Creating, editing or
//      deleting an annotation NEVER advances its target's revision, emits no change
//      event on the target, and never enters the target's content hash. The
//      annotation carries its own identity, revision history and provenance; its
//      OWN revision advances normally. This is not a special rule — it falls out of
//      the target being untouched (an annotation is a separate resource; the target
//      does not name it), under Core's revision rule: "the revision changes exactly
//      when the resource's state changes" ([[core.lumen#Rev]]).
//   2. Point AT, don't own — and point SOFTLY. An annotation is a first-class
//      resource that names its target with a `target: ref<resource>` property. That
//      reference is SOFT, not structural: it tolerates a retired target and resolves
//      to its tombstone, and the target never cascades its retirement to the
//      annotation (contrast a `rel<>`'s structural `source`/`target`, which a scope
//      cascades or restricts — [[core.lumen#rel]]). The target's view of its
//      annotations (issue.lumen `labels` / `comments`) is a COMPUTED projection.
//   3. Projection is a reverse lookup, revision-transparent. A target's `labels` /
//      `comments` are computed by walking the `target` reference in REVERSE from the
//      target. Computing one never advances the target's revision, emits no event,
//      and never enters its content hash.
// =============================================================================

/// description: Free text — a nominal string.
type text is string


// #############################################################################
// §1. The model
// #############################################################################

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §1.0  RESOLVED — annotation is a resource that SOFT-references its target,
//       NOT a `rel<>` edge.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The #403 record left open whether an Annotation is better carried as a
// `rel<resource, …>` edge (like issue.lumen's Dependency) or as a resource with a
// `target` property. It is a resource with a `target` property. Three reasons, each
// grounded in Core:
//
//   1. A `rel<F, T>` is defined by its STRUCTURAL `source`/`target` pair — "nothing
//      distinguishes a relationship but its structural `source`/`target` pair"
//      ([[core.lumen#rel]]). An annotation has exactly ONE resource reference (its
//      target) plus value payload (`body`/`author`/`name`); there is no second
//      resource endpoint — a comment's `author` is a native string, not a ref. There
//      is no `source` resource to put on the left of a `rel<>`. Modelling it as a rel
//      would mean inventing a spurious endpoint.
//
//   2. Core already classifies this exact case. A reference that only points AT a
//      resource — "a note, an annotation" — is a SOFT reference, opposed to the
//      structural `source`/`target` of an edge ([[core.lumen#rel]], verbatim
//      example). A soft reference "tolerates a retired target and resolves to its
//      tombstone"; a structural endpoint never outlives its resource. An annotation
//      wants the soft semantics: a comment on a deleted issue should survive and
//      resolve to the issue's tombstone, not be cascade-retired and not pin the
//      issue open.
//
//   3. The payload IS the point. A rel's `{ edge-fields }` are properties OF the
//      relationship (Dependency's `createdBy`/`threadId`); the relationship without
//      its endpoints is nothing. An annotation's `body`/`name` are its reason to
//      exist; the target is the thing it is attached to. That is the shape of a
//      resource-with-a-soft-ref, not an edge.
//
// Discriminator for future packs: two resources genuinely related → `rel<>` (an
// edge, structural, keyed on the pair). One resource annotated by a free-standing
// payload → an Annotation (a resource, soft ref, keyed on target + its own fields).
//
// The issue pack KEEPS `labels` / `comments` as computed projections over this
// model (PRINCIPLE 3); it does not fold them into Issue.

/// description: Annotation — a rev-neutral resource that points AT a target resource and carries its own payload. `abstract` — only the concrete annotations below are instantiated.
/// normative: Rev-neutral w.r.t. the target. Writing, editing or removing an Annotation NEVER advances its `target`'s revision, emits NO change event on the target, and NEVER enters the target's content hash. This holds because the target is not touched by the write: the Annotation is a separate resource and the target does not name it. The Annotation has its own identity, revision history and provenance; its OWN revision advances normally, and its own write DOES emit a change event on the Annotation.
/// normative: `target` is a SOFT reference ([[core.lumen#rel]]): it is a plain `ref<resource>` body property, NOT the structural `source`/`target` of a `rel<>`. It tolerates a retired target and resolves to the target's tombstone; the target's retirement is never cascaded or restricted by an Annotation. An Annotation is NOT a field on the target — a target's view of its annotations is a COMPUTED projection (see issue.lumen `labels` / `comments`), computed by walking `target` in reverse.
abstract type Annotation is Bead {
  target: ref<resource>,          // the annotated resource — a SOFT reference
}

/// description: Comment — free-text commentary on a target resource.
/// normative: `author` is a native human/agent string — NOT a BDP attribution ref. `body` is the comment text. Two Comments on the same target are distinct resources (no natural key): a target may carry many, each with its own id and history.
type Comment is Annotation {
  body:    text,
  author?: text,                  // native author string — NOT a ref
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §1.2  Label — a named tag; the natural-key question is CORE-OPEN.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: Label — a named tag on a target resource. Its natural key is (`target`, `name`): at most one Label of a given `name` on a given target, and a same-key re-add is an upsert onto the one id.
/// normative: The natural key (`target`, `name`) is a RESOURCE natural-key constraint, NOT `unique` on a rel. `unique rel<F, T>` keys ONLY on the structural (`source`, `target`) pair and explicitly excludes body fields ([[core.lumen#rel]]); Label is not a rel, and `name` is a value, not a resource endpoint — so a rel's `unique` cannot express this key. The key must be carried by the resource, over one ref property (`target`) and one value property (`name`).
/// normative: CORE-OPEN — Core has no resource-level natural-key primitive today; `unique` is defined for `rel<>` only, where it derives the id deterministically from (`source`, `target`). Spelling Label's key therefore needs ONE of: (a) Core grows a resource natural-key modifier — the resource analogue of rel `unique`, deriving the id deterministically from a declared key tuple that may mix ref and value properties (e.g. `unique(target, name)`); or (b) the at-most-one-per-(target, name) invariant is enforced at the pack/operation layer (an upsert guard on label-add), NOT by a Core-derived id. (a) is preferred — it keeps the same-key-resolves-to-one-id replica guarantee that rel `unique` already gives Dependency. The decision is the Lumen scrub's; the intent is written here with the key tuple explicit so whichever spelling lands is a mechanical substitution.
type Label is Annotation {
  name: text,                     // natural key with `target` (see CORE-OPEN above)
}


// #############################################################################
// §2. The standard operations — search / query / batch / graph (et al.)
// #############################################################################
// The cross-cutting, non-domain capabilities that sit ABOVE the core primitive
// floor. Core's floor is `get` + `list(where: Filter)` — a flat conjunction, "the
// low bar every service supports" ([[core.lumen#list]]). These ops are the
// à-la-carte capabilities a service MAY advertise on top of it:
//
//   query  — the full boolean/traversal filter Core defers to "the stdlib": the
//            rich form of list's flat Filter (nested and/or/not + edge traversal).
//   search — ranked text search (lexical / semantic / hybrid) over bodies.
//   graph  — traverse rel<> edges from roots into a subgraph; the generic form of
//            issue.lumen's getDependencyTree, over ANY relationship.
//   batch  — bundle many invocations into one request (atomic or best-effort).
//   count  — the aggregate reduction (a scalar, no bodies) issue.lumen §2.A's
//            `count` verb lowers to.
//
// (`exists` needs no op of its own — `count … > 0`. `get-many` now DOES have a
// dedicated op, §2.6 `getMany`: a `batch` of `get`s loses which ids were absent,
// and native `bd get <id>...` wants one miss-partitioned snapshot.)
//
// Each is an INTRINSIC operation (native, no stored implementation), addressed by
// its well-known path under this pack (`stdlib/query`, …) and invoked through core
// `call` ([[core.lumen#call]]). A service lists the ops it offers in discovery;
// one it does not advertise is simply absent — a floor service offers none of
// these and `get` + `list` still work. The pack mounts its commands at the TOP of
// the aggregate `bd` tree (`cli.mount: "/"`), and each op carries a simple
// `cli.name`, so the catalog renders `bd query` / `bd search` / `bd graph` /
// `bd batch` / `bd count` directly ([[core.lumen#discovery]]). The service's bounds
// — supported `SearchMode`s, whether `via` traversal is offered, `maxGraphDepth`,
// `maxBatch` — ride in discovery as this pack's capability metadata.
//
// Error model (as §2.0 of issue.lumen): a safe query (`fn`) reads or faults with a
// `Problem` (malformed input, an unsupported capability) and declares no domain
// `E`; `batch` (`command`) carries each item's own `Outcome` and needs no
// top-level `E`. (`Scope` / `Filter` / `listCondition` / `selector` / `ReadRequest`
// / `Outcome` / `rep` / `source` / `PathString` / `FieldPath` are core types.)
//
// Pack fault codes (Problem `code`): `query-capability-unsupported` (a `via` traversal
// the service does not offer), `search-mode-unsupported` (an unoffered `SearchMode`),
// `match-mode-unsupported` (an unoffered `matches.using`, e.g. `regex`),
// `graph-depth-exceeded` (depth past `maxGraphDepth`), `limit-exceeded` (a batch past
// `maxBatch` — core's existing 422 static-bound code, NOT a new `batch-*` code),
// `nested-batch-unsupported` (a batch item whose op is `stdlib/batch`),
// `atomic-unsupported` (an atomic batch before the atomic lowering lands),
// `referential-integrity` (a `deleteResource onIncident: fail` would leave an inbound soft
// reference dangling), `capability-not-supported` (a `deleteResource` mode needing
// inbound-reference tracking the scope does not advertise).

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §2.1  query — list's rich sibling
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: The full query predicate — the rich form of list's flat [[core.lumen#Filter]]. A tree of boolean combinators over the same leaf conditions, plus edge traversal. `cond` reuses the base `listCondition` verbatim, so every `where: Filter` is a degenerate `all` of `cond`s — floor and capability speak ONE condition language.
/// normative: Wire encoding — internally-tagged with a `kind` discriminator (the convention of [[core.lumen#Outcome]] / [[core.lumen#listCondition]]): `{"kind":"all","of":[…]}`, `{"kind":"any","of":[…]}`, `{"kind":"not","expr":…}`, `{"kind":"cond","condition":<listCondition>}`, `{"kind":"via","edge":…,"direction":"out"|"in","where"?:…}`. `cond.condition` carries a core `listCondition` VERBATIM (same kind-tagged, root-addressed `FieldPath` array encoding). So a flat `where: Filter` [c₁, c₂] is exactly `{"kind":"all","of":[{"kind":"cond","condition":c₁},{"kind":"cond","condition":c₂}]}` — one condition language across `list` and `query`.
type QueryExpr = union {
  /// description: Conjunction — every sub-expression must hold (an empty `of` is vacuously true).
  all  { of: [ QueryExpr ] }
  /// description: Disjunction — at least one sub-expression must hold.
  any  { of: [ QueryExpr ] }
  /// description: Negation.
  not  { expr: QueryExpr }
  /// description: A leaf — one base field condition ([[core.lumen#listCondition]]).
  cond { condition: listCondition }
  /// description: Traverse a relationship and match on the far end — holds when a neighbor reached over `edge` (a `rel<>` type-path) in `direction` satisfies `where`. The generic "has a neighbor such that" test.
  via  { edge: PathString, direction: "out" | "in" = "out", where?: QueryExpr }
}

/// description: One sort key over the result set; `sort` is an ordered list (the first key is primary).
type Sort = { field: FieldPath, order: "asc" | "desc" = "asc" }

/// description: The full query — list's rich sibling. The current resources in `scope` matching a boolean/traversal `QueryExpr`, ordered by `sort`, projected by `select`. The capability form of `list(where: Filter)`; a flat `Filter` is the degenerate case. snapshot<Resource> — a FINITE cut (`--format page|jsonl`; no `sse`). (`stdlib/query`; `cli.name: "query"` → `bd query`.)
/// normative: Read-or-fault — a malformed predicate faults `malformed-query`; no domain `E`. A service advertising `query` MUST accept every boolean combinator (`all`/`any`/`not`/`cond`); `via` traversal MAY be separately capability-gated and faults `query-capability-unsupported` when the service does not offer it.
fn query(scope: Scope, where: QueryExpr, sort?: [ Sort ], select?: selector, request?: ReadRequest) -> snapshot<Resource>

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §2.2  search — ranked text search
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: How a search matches — `lexical` (substring / full-text, the floor for any `search` service), `semantic` (embedding similarity), or `hybrid` (both, fused).
type SearchMode = "lexical" | "semantic" | "hybrid"

/// description: One ranked search result — the matched resource, its relevance score (higher = better; the scale is service-defined and comparable only WITHIN one response), and an optional highlighted snippet.
type SearchHit = {
  resource: ref<resource>,
  score:    number,
  snippet?: string
}

/// description: Ranked text search over resource bodies in `scope` — the matches for `text`, best first. `where` pre-filters the corpus (the same predicate as `query`); `mode` picks lexical / semantic / hybrid; `select` expands each hit's resource inline. (`stdlib/search`; `cli.name: "search"` → `bd search`.)
/// normative: Read-or-fault — no domain `E`. `lexical` is the floor every `search` service offers; requesting a `mode` the service does not advertise faults `search-mode-unsupported`. Scores order ONE response and are not comparable across calls or services.
/// normative: `search` returns a ranked `[SearchHit]` bounded by `limit` — it is NOT a `source<Resource>` sequence and does NOT use the `Page` cursor envelope (a caller wants the top matches, not a resumable full stream). `graph`'s `Subgraph` is likewise a bounded STRUCTURE, not a Page. The unified `Page` read envelope ([[core.lumen#Page]]) covers only the ordered resumable reads (list / query / pull / revisions / export). Cursoring `search` is a possible future extension, not v0.
/// cli.name: "search"
fn search(
    scope:   Scope,
    /// cli.positional: 0
    text:    string,
    /// cli.flag: --where
    where?:  QueryExpr,
    /// cli.flag: --mode
    mode?:   SearchMode = "lexical",
    /// cli.flag: --select
    select?: selector,
    /// cli.alias: -n
    limit?:  integer
  ) -> [ SearchHit ]

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §2.3  graph — relationship traversal
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: One resource reached by a traversal, with the fewest hops from any root at which it was found.
type GraphNode = { resource: ref<resource>, depth: integer }

/// description: One relationship traversed — the edge resource itself and its endpoints, denormalized so a client renders the graph without re-reading each edge.
type GraphEdge = {
  edge:   ref<resource>,
  type:   PathString,
  source: ref<resource>,
  target: ref<resource>
}

/// description: The result of a `graph` traversal — the reachable nodes and the edges among them. A node appears ONCE (lowest depth); an edge that closes a cycle is still reported, so the shape is the actual graph, not a spanning tree.
type Subgraph = { nodes: [ GraphNode ], edges: [ GraphEdge ] }

/// description: Traverse relationships from one or more roots into a `Subgraph`. `edgeTypes` restricts which `rel<>` types to follow (type-path prefix; absent = all); `direction` follows `source→target` (`out`), `target→source` (`in`), or `both`; `depth` caps the hops. The generic form of issue.lumen's `getDependencyTree`, over any `rel<>`. (`stdlib/graph`; `cli.name: "graph"` → `bd graph`.)
/// normative: Read-or-fault — no domain `E`. Traversal is breadth-first and visits each node once, so a cyclic graph terminates and a cycle-closing edge is still emitted. `depth` is bounded by the advertised `maxGraphDepth`; a request above it faults `graph-depth-exceeded` rather than silently truncating.
/// cli.name: "graph"
fn graph(
    /// cli.positional: 0
    roots:      [ ref<resource> ],
    /// cli.flag: --edge-types
    edgeTypes?: [ PathString ],
    /// cli.flag: --direction
    direction?: "out" | "in" | "both" = "out",
    /// cli.alias: -d
    /// cli.flag: --depth
    depth?:     integer = 1,
    /// cli.flag: --select
    select?:    selector
  ) -> Subgraph

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §2.4  batch — many invocations, one request
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: One invocation inside a `batch` — the op to run (a primitive name like `create`/`patch`/`delete`, or an operation path like `issue/claim`) and its `arguments` (the same name/value shape as [[core.lumen#Request]]), which carry EVERY input including the subject/target resource as a named value. `id` is a caller label echoed on the matching result.
/// normative: There is NO separate `target` field — a resource-targeted op names its subject as an ordinary argument (`{"name":"issue","value":{"id":"a"}}`), exactly as `call`'s `Request.arguments` does, so the generic router needs no per-op "which arg is the target" knowledge. The CLI MAY sugar a positional target onto the right named arg via the op's `cli` hints.
type BatchItem = {
  id:        string,
  op:        PathString,
  arguments: [ { name: string, value: any } ]
}

/// description: The fate of one `BatchItem`, correlated by `id` — the item's own `Outcome` (success / failure / fault / canceled).
type BatchResult = { id: string, outcome: Outcome<any, any> }

/// description: Run many invocations in one request. `atomic: false` (default) runs the items independently and best-effort — each result stands alone and partial success is normal. `atomic: true` runs them in one transaction — all commit or none. Items apply in array order. (`stdlib/batch`; `cli.name: "batch"` → `bd batch`.)
/// normative: The call returns `200` with one `BatchResult` per item, keyed by `id`; the HTTP status reports the batch TRANSPORT, never an item's fate (read each item's `outcome`). ATOMIC rollback: if any item settles as other than `success`, NOTHING commits, and every result reports either the triggering `failure`/`fault` or — for the siblings rolled back — `canceled { reason: "rolled-back" }`. So "did the batch commit?" is exactly "is every outcome a success?". A whole-request fault (malformed, or past `maxBatch`) faults with a `Problem` and returns no results. v0.2: items target ONE scope; cross-scope batching is out of scope.
/// normative: Per-item faults are OUTCOME-WRAPPED — a failing item's `outcome` is the `fault`/`failure`/`canceled` arm (`{"kind":"fault","problem":…}`), because `batch` (like `call`) reports each result as an `Outcome`. This differs from a DIRECT `fn`/primitive call, which faults out-of-band as a BARE `Problem` (no Outcome envelope). A whole-request `batch` fault is likewise a bare `Problem`: past `maxBatch` → `limit-exceeded`, a nested `stdlib/batch` item → `nested-batch-unsupported`, `atomic:true` before the atomic lowering lands → `atomic-unsupported`.
command batch(items: [ BatchItem ], atomic?: bool = false) -> [ BatchResult ]

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §2.5  count — the aggregate reduction
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: The number of current resources in `scope` matching `where` — the aggregate reduction of `query`/`list`, computed service-side (no bodies returned). The op issue.lumen §2.A's `count` verb lowers to. (`stdlib/count`; `cli.name: "count"` → `bd count`.)
/// normative: Read-or-fault — no domain `E`. `where` is the full `QueryExpr`; a service offering only base `list` MAY accept a flat `Filter` (a degenerate `all` of `cond`s) and fault `query-capability-unsupported` on richer predicates.
/// cli.name: "count"
fn count(
    scope:  Scope,
    /// cli.flag: --where
    where?: QueryExpr
  ) -> integer

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §2.6  getMany — the miss-partitioned bulk point-read
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: Miss-partitioned bulk point-read — resolve many refs within one scope against a single consistent snapshot, returned as `found` + `missing`. A plain batch of `get`s loses which ids were absent and the alias→target correlation; `getMany` returns the miss set explicitly and tags each hit with the ref the caller passed. Each ref resolves exactly as [[core.lumen#get]] (the alias overlay, a pinned `rev`), and `select` projects each hit. (`stdlib/getMany`; `cli.name: "get"` → `bd get <id>...`.)
/// normative: A ref is partitioned by how it RESOLVES (as [[core.lumen#get]]): a ref that resolves — including a pinned `rev` naming a tombstone revision, or a pinned earlier live revision — is a `found` hit; an UNPINNED ref whose target is retired is `missing` with `retired: true` (the 410 case); a ref that does not resolve (never existed, or a pinned `rev` that does not exist) is `missing` without `retired` (the 404 case). On a `reusable`/`unversioned` scope retired and never-existed collapse, so `retired` is unreachable there, exactly as core `get`'s 410 is.
/// normative: Read-or-fault — a `Problem` is raised only for a WHOLE-request error: malformed, past `maxBatch` → `limit-exceeded`, or a ref naming a scope other than the call's (`getMany` is single-scope). An absent or retired ref is NEVER a whole-request fault. `found` and `missing` preserve request order; a duplicate ref resolves once and appears once. Every hit is read against ONE consistent snapshot; the read is non-resumable and `maxBatch`-bounded.
/// cli.name: "get"
fn getMany(
    refs:    [ ref<any> ],
    select?: selector
  ) -> GetManyResult

/// description: The result of [[stdlib.lumen#getMany]] — the hits (each tagged with the ref the caller passed) and the refs that did not resolve.
type GetManyResult = {
  /// description: The resolved hits, in request order.
  found:   [ GetManyHit ],
  /// description: The refs that did not resolve — absent, or retired (unpinned).
  missing: [ MissingRef ]
}

/// description: One resolved hit in [[stdlib.lumen#getMany]] — the input ref (so an alias resolves back to its name) and the resolved representation.
type GetManyHit = {
  /// description: The ref the caller passed (an alias name is preserved here, though `resource.id` is the canonical target).
  ref:      ref<any>,
  /// description: The resolved representation, projected by `select`.
  resource: Resource
}

/// description: One ref that did not resolve in [[stdlib.lumen#getMany]].
type MissingRef = {
  /// description: The ref that did not resolve.
  ref:      ref<any>,
  /// description: True when the target is retired (a tombstone, unpinned 410); absent when the target or pinned revision does not exist (404).
  retired?: bool
}


// #############################################################################
// §3. Surface-coverage ops — the v0 HTTP data commands beyond core + issue
// #############################################################################
// Named entry points for the generic, portable DATA/analysis commands the native
// `bd serve` surface exposes beyond core + issue, drafted from a per-handler read.
// (Admin/ops commands — compact/gc/purge/config/… — go to ops.lumen. TWO native
// commands are deliberately NOT here: `orphans` cross-references open issues against
// GIT COMMIT messages — a product VCS-integration feature, backend/git-coupled, not a
// portable op; and `prime` is an agent-memory feature.) Several of these GENERALISE an
// issue-flavoured native command — the generic form is parameterised and the type/pack
// supplies the specifics; each is flagged for the review's placement call.
//
// >Review: `Duration` is used here and in ops.lumen but currently lives in issue.lumen
//   — it should move to CORE so the lower packs can use it without an upward dependency.

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §3.1  kv — a general key-value side-store (a RESOURCE type, no new primitives)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: A general-purpose scoped key-value entry — arbitrary string data under a dotted key, outside the domain model (native `bd kv`). Modelled as a RESOURCE: `get`/`put`/`list`/`delete` are the generic primitives, and the CLI sugars `kv get/set/list/clear` (cli.name-mounted) onto them — so no new ops are needed. Distinct from ops `config` (operational settings): kv is user DATA, and the two MUST NOT share keys.
/// normative: The `id` is the key; `value` is opaque text. Reserved key namespaces (`memory.*`, `sync.*`, `config.*`, …) are refused. A `KvEntry` is an ordinary resource (own revision + provenance), not ephemeral unless stored so.
type KvEntry is Bead {
  value: string,
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §3.2  stale — a named time-predicate query
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: Resources not updated within `olderThan` — the "possibly abandoned" view (native `bd stale`). A NAMED entry point over `query`: it composes the `updatedAt`-age predicate so callers need not hand-roll it. Generic over any resource carrying an `updatedAt`. (`stdlib/stale`; `cli.name: "stale"`.)
/// normative: Read-or-fault. The generic predicate is `updatedAt` age ONLY; a pack MAY refine it (issue additionally excludes actively-leased/claimed work and filters by status) in its own `stale`-flavoured op — those refinements do not live here. `where?` pre-filters, `limit?` caps.
fn stale(olderThan: Duration, where?: QueryExpr, limit?: integer) -> snapshot<Resource>

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §3.3  duplicates / similar — DETECTION only (never merge)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Detection only — the resolve/merge action (re-parenting edges, closing sources) is
// domain-specific and stays in the owning pack (issue). stdlib FINDS; the pack FIXES.

/// description: Exact-content duplicate GROUPS — resources whose content (a declared `fields` set) is identical, grouped (native `bd duplicates`, detection half). `fields` names the `FieldPath` set that defines "content"; a type MAY declare a default content-key as metadata. (`stdlib/duplicates`; `cli.name: "duplicates"`.)
/// normative: Read-only. Groups resources sharing an identical tuple over `fields` (a group has ≥2 members). Does NOT merge — merging is a pack op. `fields` is required unless the scoped type declares a content-key.
fn duplicates(type: PathString, fields?: [ FieldPath ], where?: QueryExpr) -> [ DuplicateGroup ]

/// description: A set of resources sharing identical content.
type DuplicateGroup = { members: [ ref<resource> ], contentKey?: string }

/// description: Similar (not identical) resource PAIRS, ranked — the fuzzy counterpart to `duplicates` (native `bd find-duplicates`). `mode` picks the scorer, `threshold` the floor. Capability-gated like `search`. (`stdlib/similar`; `cli.name: "similar"`.)
/// normative: Read-only. `mode` ∈ the service's advertised `similarityModes` (`lexical` the floor; `semantic` where offered — an unoffered mode faults `similarity-mode-unsupported`). Scores order ONE response only. A `semantic` mode MAY call an external model; a service that will not simply omits the mode.
fn similar(type: PathString, mode?: string = "lexical", threshold?: number, fields?: [ FieldPath ], where?: QueryExpr, limit?: integer) -> [ SimilarPair ]

/// description: One scored pair of similar resources.
type SimilarPair = { a: ref<resource>, b: ref<resource>, score: number, reason?: string }

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §3.4  lint — validate resources against their type's declared requirements
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: Validate resources against their TYPE's declared requirements and report what is missing/ill-formed — never auto-fixing (native `bd lint`, which only reports). The requirements are TYPE-declared metadata (e.g. an issue subtype's required template sections); `lint` is the generic checker that reads them. (`stdlib/lint`; `cli.name: "lint"`.)
/// normative: Read-only — reports, never mutates; a clean resource yields no findings. The rule source is type-declared metadata (a pack ships its rules on its types) — stdlib supplies the ENGINE, not the rules. `where?` scopes the set.
fn lint(where?: QueryExpr, limit?: integer) -> [ LintResult ]

/// description: One resource's lint findings — the resource and its list of issues (missing sections, warnings).
type LintResult = { resource: ref<resource>, findings: [ string ] }

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §3.5  deleteResource — the multi-id delete facade (cascade / sever / dry-run)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: Multi-id retire with a chosen inbound-reference disposition and an optional dry run — the stdlib facade over the core [[core.lumen#delete]] primitive (the generic form of native `bd delete <id>...`, and of v0's issue-flavoured bulk delete). `delete` retires ONE resource and lets inbound refs dangle; `deleteResource` adds the array request shape and the cascade / sever / fail dispositions the CLI needs, while the wire `delete` stays minimal. Guards and modes ride as call ARGUMENTS (it is a callable, not an HTTP method), not conditional headers. (`stdlib/deleteResource`; `cli.name: "delete"` → `bd delete`.)
/// normative: Each target is retired as by [[core.lumen#delete]] — a tombstone revision (`retired: true`, `body: null`), idempotent. `onIncident` governs SOFT inbound references (a `ref<T>` body property that points AT a retired resource, e.g. an Annotation); STRUCTURAL `rel<>` endpoints are NOT governed here — a relationship's own cascade/restrict on its `source`/`target` is core behaviour ([[core.lumen#rel]]) and runs regardless. `dangle` leaves soft refs to resolve to the tombstone (the core default); `fail` refuses when a soft ref would be left dangling; `sever` retires the resources that HOLD the inbound soft ref (listed in `severed`); `cascade` transitively retires resources reachable by inbound soft refs (listed per target in `retired`). `fail`/`sever`/`cascade` require inbound-reference tracking (an advertised capability, [[core.lumen#delete]]); a scope offering `deleteResource` without it faults `capability-not-supported` on those modes.
/// normative: Guards match the `delete` primitive and apply to the explicitly requested `refs`, not to cascaded dependents: `precondition` filters each target; `ifRevision` is SINGLE-ref only — supplied with `len(refs) ≠ 1` it faults `400` malformed. A target failing `precondition` or `ifRevision` settles `blocked`, not a whole-request fault.
/// normative: The call settles per-ref as a `DeleteOutcome` (`200`, one per requested target in request order). Non-`fail` modes are BEST-EFFORT: a `blocked`/`not-found` target does not abort its siblings (partial commit is normal — read each outcome's `status`), unlike the sibling [[stdlib.lumen#batch]] whose `atomic` arm is all-or-nothing; `DeleteOutcome` is a deliberate per-ref lifecycle shape, not an `Outcome`. `onIncident: fail` is the ONE whole-request mode: if any target would leave an inbound soft ref dangling, the call faults `referential-integrity` and NOTHING commits. `dryRun: true` computes the full outcome WITHOUT committing — a target that would be retired reports `would-delete`, others report `blocked`/`skipped`/`not-found`; a dry run under `onIncident: fail` reports the would-block targets as `blocked` and does NOT raise `referential-integrity`, so the preview stays usable. A never-existed target settles `not-found` (on a `reusable`/`unversioned` scope, indistinguishable from `skipped`, as core `delete`'s 404 is unreachable there).
/// cli.name: "delete"
command deleteResource(
    refs:          [ unpinned<any> ],
    /// description: Disposition for SOFT inbound references when a target is retired — `dangle` (leave them to resolve to the tombstone, the core default), `fail` (refuse the whole request if any soft ref would be left dangling), `sever` (retire the resources holding the inbound soft ref), `cascade` (transitively retire resources reachable by inbound soft refs).
    onIncident?:   OnIncident = "dangle",
    /// description: Compute the full outcome — every tombstone and affected reference — without committing.
    dryRun?:       bool = false,
    /// description: Revision guard; SINGLE-ref only (faults `400` when `len(refs) ≠ 1`). Retire only if the one target still matches this revision; else that target is `blocked`.
    ifRevision?:   Rev,
    /// description: State guard. Retire a target only if it satisfies this Filter; else that target is `blocked`.
    precondition?: Filter
  ) -> [ DeleteOutcome ]

/// description: Inbound-reference disposition for [[stdlib.lumen#deleteResource]]; see the op's normative for each mode.
type OnIncident = "dangle" | "fail" | "sever" | "cascade"

/// description: The fate of one requested target in [[stdlib.lumen#deleteResource]].
type DeleteOutcome = {
  /// description: The target this outcome is for.
  ref:      ref<any>,
  /// description: `deleted` (tombstone minted), `would-delete` (a `dryRun` projection of a real retirement), `skipped` (idempotent — already retired), `not-found` (never existed), or `blocked` (a guard or an `onIncident` disposition refused it).
  status:   "deleted" | "would-delete" | "skipped" | "not-found" | "blocked",
  /// description: The tombstone revision — minted now on `deleted`, or the existing one on `skipped` (matching `delete`'s idempotent return).
  rev?:     Rev,
  /// description: Every id retired for this target — the target itself plus, under `cascade`, its transitive soft-ref dependents; in a `dryRun`, what WOULD be retired (unpinned refs, since no rev is minted).
  retired?: [ ref<any> ],
  /// description: The resources retired under `sever` because they held an inbound soft reference to this target.
  severed?: [ ref<any> ],
  /// description: Why, when `blocked`, `skipped`, or `not-found`.
  reason?:  string
}

// CLI arity-dispatch: `bd get` / `bd delete` route a SINGLE id to the core primitive
// (`get` → `rep`, `delete` → `Rev`) and MULTIPLE ids to these stdlib ops (`getMany` →
// `GetManyResult`, `deleteResource` → `[DeleteOutcome]`); the output shape follows the
// arity. A client whose scope does not advertise stdlib fans the multi-id form out to N
// core primitives and synthesizes the partition / outcome list itself.
